  "background": "Hebrews does not name its human author. Early Christians proposed several possibilities, and later tradition often associated the work with Paul, but the letter itself remains anonymous. The recipients, location, and exact date are also debated. The text appears to address a Christian community deeply formed by Israel’s Scriptures and experiencing pressure, weariness, and the temptation to drift from public allegiance to Christ. Hebrews 13:22 calls the work a “word of exhortation,” which fits its blend of sermon-like exposition, warning, encouragement, and closing personal remarks.\n\nHebrews reasons almost entirely from Israel’s Scriptures and speaks within a story that includes temple worship, priesthood, covenant, sacrifice, wilderness, kingship, and promise. Jesus, the apostles, and the earliest church were Jewish. Therefore language such as “better covenant,” “obsolete,” “shadow,” or “better priesthood” must be read as the writer’s Christ-centered interpretation of God’s own covenant history, not as permission for antisemitism, contempt for Judaism, caricature of the Old Testament, or claims that Jewish people are spiritually inferior.",
